# Stage 1: example B1 with a proton beam, everything P9-P13 owes inactivated,
# and Decay and hadElastic LEFT ACTIVE.
#
# B1's own hadronic beam. 210 MeV is the energy docs/RESULT.md's headline comparison uses:
# the proton stops inside the scoring volume, so this row is a Bragg-peak dose and not a
# plateau one - which makes it the most sensitive of the nine to anything that changes a
# range. The proton is STABLE, so `Decay` is not on its manager at all and there is
# nothing for stage 1 to switch on for it: what this row measures is hadElastic alone.
#
# Every name below is a row of ref/oracle/species_processes.csv for proton - the dump of
# the constructed QBBC's own process manager. /process/inactivate ignores a name the
# species does not have, silently, so a macro written from the source rather than from
# the object can look like it switched something off. docs/RISK.md V43.

/run/initialize
/control/verbose 0
/run/verbose 1

/process/inactivate hBrems
/process/inactivate hPairProd
/process/inactivate protonInelastic

# The stage, recorded by what RAN and not by what was intended. docs/RISK.md V43's
# lesson: ask the object. This prints Active/InActive for every process on the
# particle's manager, after the inactivations above.
/particle/select proton
/particle/process/dump

/gun/particle proton
/gun/energy 210 MeV

/run/printProgress 100000
/run/beamOn 500000
